Cause: this whole session's container runs every shell command as root
(confirmed via `whoami`/`id`; Solr itself needed `bin/solr start --force`
for the same reason). OpenSearch's own bootstrap (Bootstrap.
initializeNatives) explicitly checks whether it is running as root and
refuses if so -- a genuine safety check present in OpenSearch's own
code, not a JVM/environment restriction. Elasticsearch's equivalent
check (P6E-E00) did NOT hard-fail under the identical root+no-native-
access condition -- it logged "Cannot check if running as root because
native access is not available" and continued, meaning the same
underlying condition (native access library unavailable in this
container, e.g. no zstd native lib) is handled differently by the two
forks: ES treats "can't determine" as non-fatal, OpenSearch treats it as
fatal. This is a real, disclosed divergence between the two forks'
bootstrap code, not an inconsistency in this project's own testing.

Fixed by running the OpenSearch benchmark as a genuine non-root user
(the container's existing `ubuntu` uid=1000 account) rather than as
root -- an isolated copy of the module plus a separate Maven local
repository under /tmp (both chowned to ubuntu) were used, since /root
itself is mode 700 and unreadable by any other user, so ubuntu cannot
share the root-owned ~/.m2 cache. No source-code or policy changes were
needed once running as a real non-root user; installing the actual
native zstd library and re-testing as root was considered and rejected
as a workaround, since it would only convert this "can't determine, so
refuse" error into a definitive "is root, refusing" error -- the
underlying incompatibility (OpenSearch refuses to run its own bootstrap
as root, full stop) would remain.
